1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
 Now this lecture going to look at another Amazon

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:06,960
 bedrock capability called as bedrock prompt management.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:09,300
 Amazon bedrock prompt management

4
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:12,480
 simplifies the creation evaluation,

5
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:15,440
 versioning, and sharing of prompt

6
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:20,120
 to get better response from the foundation models.

7
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,480
 So in summary, Amazon bedrock prompt management

8
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:27,040
 allows you to create prompt templates, evaluate them,

9
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,480
 do the versioning, and then share

10
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:33,040
 those prompts across the organization.

11
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,240
 Now let's try and understand how you can use

12
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,240
 prompt template through an example.

13
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:41,720
 So this is our Gen A powered equipment SME assistant,

14
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:43,920
 where the winter bind field technician

15
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:47,160
 sends the incident report and images.

16
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:50,640
 And then we have the equipment SME at a global location

17
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:53,840
 who further sends this incident report and images

18
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:56,360
 and asks the foundation model to summarize

19
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:58,600
 the equipment logs.

20
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:02,120
 So let's assume that these are the log details

21
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:05,640
 and it has asked the foundation model to summarize.

22
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:08,280
 Now if we go back, what is our user prompt?

23
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:11,520
 Equipment log details and summarize.

24
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:14,480
 Now if we send these details to the foundation model,

25
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,480
 we know we're not going to get great results

26
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,840
 because we're not providing any context.

27
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:23,200
 So that's wherein the concept of system prompts

28
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,600
 come into play.

29
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:27,240
 So along with this user prompt, what we can send

30
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:30,360
 to the foundation model is, you are a wind turbine

31
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,760
 subject matter expert specializing

32
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:36,760
 in equipment analysis and log based troubleshooting.

33
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:39,520
 So it gives foundation model some context on the persona

34
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:44,280
 it needs to act as to respond to this user prompt.

35
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:48,200
 Then we can additionally say produce a concise one-page summary

36
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:52,440
 and highlight the key anomalies and failure patterns.

37
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:55,680
 So now we have the user prompt and we have the system prompt.

38
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:58,000
 So now together user prompt and system prompts,

39
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,160
 we're going to send to the Amazon bedrock.

40
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:03,320
 So it has the full context and the response

41
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:07,000
 that we might get here is going to be much better

42
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,280
 than just sending the user prompt.

43
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,360
 Now how does Amazon bedrock prompt management

44
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:12,800
 help in this scenario?

45
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,200
 So what you can do with the prompt managed capability

46
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:18,360
 is create these system prompts.

47
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:21,240
 So you create the system prompts in the prompt management

48
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:24,000
 then you evaluate them, you version them,

49
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,880
 then you can share these prompts across the organization.

50
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,760
 OK, that's pretty much it from this lecture.

51
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:29,720
 I'll see in the next lecture.

52
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:31,200
 Thank you.

